package com.bnpparibas.sit.fresh.rds.rds04.crf.back.domain.leverage.service;

/**
 * DELTA for ValidationDomainService — MANDATORY at box level and at question level.
 *
 * WHY: MANDATORY is not in the box-level switch, and the question-level path resolves only the
 * FORM-WIDE row. ECB needed neither — its one MANDATORY row is the form-wide checklist message and
 * every ECB adjustment is Mandatory = No. FED authors twenty-two MANDATORY rows (five field, one
 * lookup, seventeen question) and not one of them can fire today. They import clean, so nothing
 * reports it; the analyst simply gets no message.
 *
 * Both changes are additive for ECB: it authors no field-scoped and no question-scoped MANDATORY
 * row, so every existing test keeps its current answer.
 */
public final class ValidationDomainServiceMandatoryDelta {

    // ================================================================ 1. box level

    /*
     * fires(...), around line 260. One line.
     *
     *   case MANDATORY -> raw.isEmpty();
     *
     * Add it above SOURCE_EMPTY so the two read together, and keep the comment that follows —
     * they share a predicate on purpose and differ only in what they tell the analyst:
     *
     *   MANDATORY     the analyst must type this box       "Please insert a numeric value for X"
     *   SOURCE_EMPTY  FINSTAR never delivered it           points at FINSTAR, not at the box
     *
     * Same test, different cause, different wording. Authoring both on one box would be a mistake
     * (two messages for one empty box) but nothing needs to stop it: FED's boxes carry MANDATORY,
     * ECB's sources carry SOURCE_EMPTY, and no box in either workbook carries both.
     *
     * This is the change that matters most for Clara's review. A financial table is where several
     * mandatory boxes CAN be outstanding at once — five empty boxes, five messages, each clearing
     * as she fills it. The existing onInputs/onCalculated suppression already holds them apart
     * from the derived rules underneath.
     */

    // ================================================================ 2. question level

    /*
     * Where stoppedAtUnanswered(result) currently resolves the form-wide MANDATORY row, prefer a
     * row scoped to the question the walk actually stopped on:
     *
     *   private ValidationMessage mandatoryMessage(DecisionTreeDefinition definition,
     *                                              TraversalResult result) {
     *       String pendingKey = result.pendingQuestion().map(Question::key).orElse(null);
     *       ValidationMessage scoped = pendingKey == null
     *               ? null
     *               : questionScopedMessage(definition, ValidationRule.MANDATORY, pendingKey);
     *       return scoped != null ? scoped : formWideMessage(definition, ValidationRule.MANDATORY);
     *   }
     *
     * Backwards compatible by construction: ECB authors no question-scoped MANDATORY row, so the
     * lookup misses and it falls back to exactly the message it uses today.
     *
     * Leave the checklist path alone — an unsettled checklist keeps the form-wide message, which
     * is what "the message speaks for the form, so two unsettled blocks are still one alert"
     * means in the existing test.
     */

    // ================================================================ 3. one expectation to reset

    /*
     * At most ONE question-level mandatory message can ever fire, and it is worth being clear
     * about that before the UI is built.
     *
     * The walk is sequential: it cannot reach question five without question four being answered,
     * so result.pendingQuestion() is the ONLY question that can be mandatory-and-unanswered. Five
     * unanswered questions is not a state this engine can be in.
     *
     * The "several messages at once, each clearing as it is answered" behaviour is real, but it
     * belongs to the FINANCIAL TABLE, where a DATA_ENTRY question holds many mandatory boxes and
     * any number of them can be empty together. Change 1 above is what delivers it.
     *
     * The seventeen question-scoped rows are still worth having: they name the question rather
     * than saying "the form is incomplete". They just arrive one at a time.
     */
}
